            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house had more air leakage than I would expect for a recently renovated home. I suspect this is mostly due to the uninsulated sections of your basement. 



I also noted some air leakage around the attic hatch. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 



I also recommend you outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 



The weather stripping around your exterior doors appeared to be in good shape. However, it should be repaired or replaced should it become damaged in the future. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $96 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA.



I do not recommend an exterior main wall insulation upgrade for your home at this time. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 2x6 framing and R20 insulation. Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation and two inches (R10) of rigid foam insulation on the exterior.



Between your crawlspace and the uninsulated wall behind your domestic hot water tank, approximately 45% of your basement is uninsulated. I recommend that you frame and insulate the space behind the hot water tank in order to reduce the heat loss from these areas, as well as adding insulation to the crawlspace walls. Your other option is to insulate the floor of your crawlspace. 



Regardless of how you choose to insulate the crawlspace, I strongly recommend that you have a contractor add ventilation to your crawlspace to allow any moisture down there to dry out. 



Savings:

By insulating the walls of your crawlspace and the space behind your hot water heater to approximately R20 and adding two inches of rigid foam EPS to the floor, you could potentially save $225 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $210 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 189 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. Since you are already using propane for heat, you could replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $1,100 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 0.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

I recommend you install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct.



I have modelled installing the pellet stove on your main floor, and using it for 25% of your heating load. I recommend speaking to a contractor or supplier to discuss options.



Savings:

By a Certified pellet stove and using it for 25% of your heating load, you could potentially save $190 per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $1,000, from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

